<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#pythia">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pythia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pythia was the priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Delphi who spoke the god's oracles. Seated on a tripod in the inner part of the temple, she answered questions from cities and individuals, and her words were then put into form by priests. Her name was linked to the serpent Python, which Apollo was said to have killed there. The office lasted for well over a thousand years, until the late 4th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pythia.jpg" alt="Pythia" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Delacroix · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eug%C3%A8ne_Delacroix_-_Lycurgus_Consulting_the_Pythia_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
